\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{Quaternion units and the complex unit}
\date{2026-11-13}

\p{The units #{i}, #{j} and #{k} in \ref{fdip-00A0} are the
quaternion units of #{\mathbb H}. Mathematical physics writes
those units #{i,j,k}, and writes #{\operatorname{Im}\mathbb H}
for their real span. Geometric algebra
(\citek{hestenes2012clifford}), which writes these units as
products of vectors, has no separate quaternion unit: it uses a
unit bivector of a Euclidean plane, or a pseudoscalar whose square
is #{-1}, for a square root of #{-1}. \ref{fdip-00A0} sends
#{\gamma_1\gamma_2}, #{\gamma_2\gamma_3} and
#{\gamma_1\gamma_3} to #{i}, #{j} and #{k}. Each product
squares to #{-\bbI}. The quaternion #{i} is not the
complex unit #{\ui}. The right complex structure in the
proposition is the real span of #{1} and that quaternion
#{i}.}
